<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emma of Normandy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 985–1052</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emma of Normandy was a daughter of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> of Normandy who was twice queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, first as wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a> from 1002 and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cnut-the-great">Cnut the Great</a> from 1017. She was the mother of the kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a>. She commissioned the Encomium Emmae Reginae, a work in praise of herself and her family, and remained a powerful figure at court until her death in 1052.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emma-of-normandy.jpg" alt="Emma of Normandy" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emma_Receiving_the_Encomium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emma was a daughter of the Norman ruler Richard the Fearless and Gunnor. King Æthelred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> married her in 1002 to pacify Normandy, since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids on England had often been based there, and her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-ii-duke-of-normandy">Richard II, Duke of Normandy</a>, hoped to improve relations with the English. On marriage she took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> name Ælfgifu, used in formal and official matters, and received properties of her own in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, Rutland, Devonshire, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxfordshire">Oxfordshire</a>, as well as the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exeter">Exeter</a>. She had two sons with Æthelred, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-the-confessor">Edward the Confessor</a> and Alfred Ætheling, and a daughter, Godgifu. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> conquered England in 1013 she and her children went to Normandy, returning after Sweyn died in 1014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Æthelred died in 1016, and Cnut, Sweyn's son, held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> until arrangements were made for him to marry Emma; she had tried to maintain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> control of the city. Some scholars think the marriage saved her sons' lives. They married in 1017, and she became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> in 1018 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1028. Her sons by Æthelred were sent to live in Normandy. With Cnut she had a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>, and a daughter, Gunhilda.</p><p>When Cnut died in 1035 Emma backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a>, who was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-harefoot">Harold Harefoot</a>. In 1036 she called Alfred and Edward back from Normandy. Alfred was captured by Earl Godwin, blinded and died of his wounds, and Edward was met by armed opposition and fled. Emma went into exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruges">Bruges</a>. Harthacnut became king in 1040, and after his death in 1042 Edward succeeded; in 1043 he accused her of treason and took her lands and titles, but soon relented and restored them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Emma died in 1052 and was buried alongside Cnut and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harthacnut">Harthacnut</a> in the Old Minster, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, and later moved to the new cathedral. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a> her remains were scattered by parliamentary forces and later reburied. Until 1043, writes Pauline Stafford, she was the richest woman in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><p>She is the central figure of the Encomium Emmae Reginae, a praise of the queen and a critical source for the politics of the early 11th century. It names Æthelred only as a prince and does not mention that she had been his wife. Scholars now read it as an intervention in the politics of the Anglo-Danish court. A portrait in the New Minster Liber Vitae shows Cnut and Emma presenting a cross to the altar.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emma of Normandy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Harold Godwinson, Earl of Wessex and the most powerful noble in England, was Edward's brother-in-law; William, Duke of Normandy, was the great-nephew of Edward's mother, <b>Emma of Normandy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of Æthelred the Unready and <b>Emma of Normandy</b>, was crowned king of the English in Winchester Cathedral, having succeeded his half-brother Harthacnut in 1042.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of Æthelred the Unready and <b>Emma of Normandy</b>, succeeded his half-brother Harthacnut as king of the English.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Cnut ruled mainly from England, often from Winchester, keeping English institutions, issuing law codes and marrying <b>Emma of Normandy</b>, widow of Æthelred the Unready.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-sea-empire">North Sea Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Normandy, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1028" aria-label="Normandy on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4540 450 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Emma of Normandy"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4990 720h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4990 720h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.12,-0.44&amp;year=1028">What was here in 1028? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
